course: dictionary
lesson: 8136
title: Report: a word with two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Report is one of those words that can do two jobs. As a noun, a report is a piece of writing or a talk that gives facts, like a school report or a weather report. As a verb, to report means to tell people about something that happened.
explain: Context shows you which job the word is doing. «Jonathan, a giant tortoise on St Helena, is reported to be around 190 years old.» Here is reported is a verb. Someone has told people this, so it is said to be true, and the word is also used to show where the fact came from.
explain: Look at the words around it. In «Nine-year-old Ella Brown rescued six ducklings from a drain on Monday», the first paragraph of a news report tells who, what, when and where. If you read «Quotes like "It was terrifying," said Mrs Patel» in a report, the words of real people make the report feel true.
fact: Report can be a noun (a report, reports) or a verb (report, reported, reporting).
fact: A school report is a paper that tells parents how a child is doing at school.
fact: To report means to tell people about something that happened.
fact: To report a problem means to tell someone in charge, like a teacher or the police.
fact: Telling a trusted adult about bullying, accidents, strangers or online problems is not tattling. It keeps people safe.
exercise: «Nine-year-old Ella Brown rescued six ducklings from a drain on Monday.» Find and copy the word that tells you when. | Ella | *Monday | ducklings | drain
exercise: «Jonathan, a giant tortoise on St Helena, is reported to be around 190 years old.» What does reported mean here? | He is good at writing long reports | *People have said so, so it is reported as a fact | He is a reporter on the island | He has been given a school report card
exercise: Ruby sees older children picking on a boy at break time. What should Ruby do? | Join in so she fits in with the group | Keep quiet, because telling is tattling | Post a video of it online and say nothing | *Tell a trusted adult, like a teacher
test: «Quotes like "It was terrifying," said Mrs Patel» in a news report do what? | *Give real people's words and make the report feel true | Hide the facts from the reader | Show that the writer is angry | Make the report shorter
test: What does a lab report explain? | *The question, method, results and conclusion | Only the pictures, with no words | A shopping list for the science lab | Jokes and riddles for the class
exam: What is a weather report? | A paper that tells parents how a child is doing at school | *A piece of writing or talk that gives facts about the weather | A type of cloud in the sky | A way to tell a teacher about a fight
